<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fakhr al-Din II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1572–<a href="/years/1635">1635</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Fakhr al-Din ibn Ma'n</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fakhr al-Din II was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> emir of the Ma'n family who governed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chouf">Shuf</a> mountains and, as an Ottoman tax farmer, extended his authority over much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and the coast. After an Ottoman expedition against him he spent 1613–1618 in exile in Tuscany. His growing power alarmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, and he was captured in 1633 and executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1635. He is often seen as a forerunner of modern Lebanon.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fakhr-al-din-ii.jpg" alt="Fakhr al-Din II" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Mariti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fakhr_al-Din_Portrait_Cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
